{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Environment Secrets Guard",
  "description": "When the .env file is edited, immediately warns about the security implications and validates that it's still in .gitignore, contains only expected key names, and doesn't accidentally have real keys in committed example files.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileEdited",
    "patterns": [
      ".env",
      ".env.example",
      ".env.local"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "An environment file was just modified. Please perform these critical checks:\n\n1. **Is .env in .gitignore?** Verify the .gitignore file contains `.env` entry. If not, this is a HIGH PRIORITY fix.\n\n2. **If .env.example was edited**: Ensure no REAL secret values were accidentally added (should only contain placeholder text like 'your-key-here' or empty values)\n\n3. **Key naming consistency**: All keys should be UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n\n4. **Expected keys only**: The framework expects these keys:\n   - OPENAI_API_KEY\n   - BROWSERSTACK_USERNAME / BROWSERSTACK_ACCESS_KEY\n   - BROWSERSTACK_ANDROID_APP_URL / BROWSERSTACK_IOS_APP_URL\n   - LAMBDATEST_USERNAME / LAMBDATEST_ACCESS_KEY\n   - LAMBDATEST_ANDROID_APP_URL / LAMBDATEST_IOS_APP_URL\n   - SAUCE_USERNAME / SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY\n   Flag any unexpected keys that might indicate scope creep or misconfiguration.\n\n5. **No real values in .env.example**: If you can read .env.example, verify all values are placeholders.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT echo back actual secret values in your response. Reference them by key name only."
  }
}